      'With your own game files, the maps of the Underground Passage, the Spider Forest, the Flayer Jungle, Kurast, Act IV and the ice caves are made again by themselves, those alone; the campaign’s Chaos Sanctuary has a map of its own.',
      'Campaign: the waypoint no longer has a “Rush” tab (no more jumping to Baal or Diablo from the campaign); it shows only the acts open (Act I, then each act whose previous boss is dead) and no level is required for a waypoint you touched, as in Diablo II. Harrogath keeps its Rush menu.',
      'Campaign: Warriv, Meshif and Tyrael take you only where Diablo II’s do: Warriv to Lut Gholein once Andariel is dead (and back to the camp), Meshif to Kurast once Duriel is dead (and back to Lut Gholein), Tyrael to Harrogath once Diablo is dead. Before that they only talk. With Mephisto dead, a red portal leads to the Pandemonium Fortress.',
      'Campaign: Diablo II’s passages guarded by a quest stay shut until the quest: Tristram (the Tree of Inifuss in the Dark Wood gives a scroll, Akara deciphers it, its key opens the Cairn Stones), Lut Gholein’s palace (the Tainted Sun), the Canyon of the Magi (the Summoner), Duriel’s Lair door (the Horadric Staff in the orifice), the Durance of Hate (Khalim’s Will), Nihlathak’s Temple (Anya freed) and the Worldstone Keep (the Ancients). A shut passage tells you what to do. Your characters keep open the passages they already went through.',
      'Home screen: the game mode is chosen in two steps. First Normal (campaign and endgame) or Survivals (one-act runs), then the game: Project Diablo 2 or Diablo II (original), with a Back button (or Escape). A few words under each button. Characters, saves and the last mode picked do not change: it is still marked at launch.',
      'Campaign: the waypoints of the Jail (level 1), the Halls of the Dead (level 2), the Arcane Sanctuary, Lower and Upper Kurast, the Durance of Hate (level 2), the City of the Damned, the River of Flame, the Halls of Pain and the Worldstone Keep (level 2) finally have their Diablo II room, with its pad on the ground (the stone no longer floats over the plain floor). The River of Flame leads to the Chaos Sanctuary over its bridge, where its waypoint stands. The Blood Moor, the Tamoe Highland, the Rocky Waste and the Worldstone Keep (level 1) no longer show the pad of a waypoint they do not have in Diablo II. With your own game files, those maps are made again by themselves, those alone.',
      'Campaign, Act II: the true Tomb of Tal Rasha’s door finally leads to Duriel’s Lair; with Duriel dead, Act III opens. Act V: the Throne of Destruction’s portal leads to the Worldstone Chamber; with Baal dead, Nightmare (then Hell) opens. Both led nowhere in the Campaign.',
      'Campaign: areas keep their state during the game, as in Diablo II. An area you leave (stairs, exit, waypoint, death) is found again as you left it: monsters killed stay dead, chests opened, items on the ground. It is made again the next time the game is launched or when you change difficulty.',
      'Campaign: no more full heal at every stairway, cave, waypoint or when entering town: your life and mana stay as they are. Heal with potions or at the town’s healer (Akara, Fara, Ormus, Jamella, Malah; poison and your mercenary too). Fara now heals and Atma does not, as in Diablo II. Gaining a level still heals.',
