      'Campaign: dying costs part of the gold you carry, as in Diablo II (your level in percent, at most 20%, the first 500 gold per level spared); no item is lost and the portal to where you died stays. The death screen names the act’s town instead of Harrogath.',
      'Campaign: the waypoints look like Diablo II’s (the stone and its blue flames, lit once the waypoint is touched); the grey pillar standing on them is gone. They stand at their exact place in the map, in every act.',
      'Campaign: no more town portal button (in town as in the zones): you go from one zone to the next on foot, through their passages, and go home by the waypoints. The button stays in Rush and the endgame maps.',
      'Campaign, Act I: the zones are joined on the side Diablo II puts them: the camp’s north gate opens onto the Blood Moor’s south, the Cold Plains are east of the Moor, the Stony Field north of the Plains, and so on up to the Monastery Gate, now reached on foot from the north of the Tamoe Highland. Each passage leads onto a real path (not a closed nook of the border), cleared of the trees that hid it.',
      'Campaign, Act I: far fewer trees in the Blood Moor (about three in ten kept) and fewer in the Cold Plains, the Black Marsh, the Tamoe Highland and the Dark Wood: clearings and copses readable on a phone, the border’s forest kept. No path is closed by it.',
      'Campaign: no more town portal opening by itself when you arrive in a zone (by a waypoint, stairs or an exit), as in Diablo II. Rush, the endgame maps and Survivals keep theirs.',
